<Base title="Advanced Sorting · ITC 120" description="Merge sort, quick sort, and heap sort: the recursion they need, how to trace each one by hand, the Java code, how much work they save, and when to use which." course={{ code: course.code, href: base + course.href }} toc>
  <header class="wrap lesson-hero flow">
    <p class="crumbs"><a href={base + course.href}>ITC 120</a> › Prelim › Sorting</p>
    <p class="kicker">Sorting algorithms · Lesson 2 of 2</p>
    <h1>Advanced Sorting: Merge, Quick, and Heap Sort</h1>
    <KindChips />
    <p class="lede">Three faster ways to sort big data, the one new tool they need (recursion), and how to choose among them.</p>
    <div class="goals">
      <p class="note-label">By the end, you can:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>explain <b>divide and conquer</b> and <b>recursion</b> (base case, recursive case, call stack), and trace a short recursive method;</li>
        <li>trace merge sort, quick sort, and heap sort by hand on a small array;</li>
        <li>read the three sorts in Java, find common bugs, and complete the merge step;</li>
        <li>compare all six sorts by work (about n × n / 2 versus about n × log n comparisons), extra memory, and stability, and choose one for a situation.</li>
      </ol>
    </div>
    <p class="muted small"><b>You need:</b> the <a href={base + '/itc120/sorting-basic/'}>Basic sorting</a> lesson (swap, pass, comparisons, stability), the <a href={base + '/itc120/arrays/'}>Arrays</a> lesson, the <a href={base + '/itc120/programming-refresher/'}>Programming refresher</a> (methods, loops, <code>&amp;&amp;</code>), and scratch paper.</p>
  </header>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="idea">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 1 · The idea</p>
      <h2>Why we need faster sorts</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Bubble, selection, and insertion sort are easy, but slow on big data: their work grows like a <Peek label="staircase" title="From Basic sorting" html={stairsPeek} />, about n × n / 2 comparisons. For the 1,000,000 transactions an e-wallet like GCash handles in a short time, that is about 500 billion comparisons. The sorts in this lesson need about 20 million.</p>
      <p>How? Think of a teacher with a big pile of quiz papers and some helpful students:</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={pileTeam()} /><figcaption>Nobody sorts the whole pile alone. Each person splits the job, and small piles are easy.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="def"><p class="note-label">Divide and conquer</p><p><b>Divide and conquer</b> is a way to solve a problem in three steps: <b>divide</b> it into smaller problems of the same kind, <b>solve</b> each smaller one (the same way), and <b>combine</b> their answers into the answer for the whole.</p></div>
      <p>Merge sort and quick sort are divide-and-conquer sorts. Heap sort uses a different trick: it keeps the values in a special shape, a <b>heap</b>, where the biggest is always easy to find. Here they are at a glance:</p>
      <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Sort</th><th>Big idea</th><th>Real-life picture</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>Merge sort</b></td><td>split in half until each part has one value, then merge sorted parts</td><td>the team of helpers above</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Quick sort</b></td><td>pick a pivot; smaller values go left, the rest go right; repeat on each side</td><td>two tables: "below 20" and "20 or more"</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b>Heap sort</b></td><td>build a heap (biggest on top), then take the biggest out again and again</td><td>a sports fest bracket: the champion is on top</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">Where you see fast sorting in IT</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Java's own <code>Arrays.sort</code></b> is a quick sort for numbers and a merge sort for objects (Part 8).</li>
          <li><b>Databases</b> sort millions of records for "ORDER BY" and use merge sort when the data does not fit in memory.</li>
          <li><b>Online shops and social apps</b> rank thousands of items by price or by time in a fraction of a second.</li>
          <li><b>Priority lists</b>, like a hospital queue where the most urgent patient goes first, use a <b>heap</b>.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="recursion">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 2 · A tool first</p>
      <h2>Recursion: a method that calls itself</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Look at the helpers again. Every helper follows the <b>same rule</b>, only on a smaller pile. In code, that means a method that calls <b>itself</b> with a smaller job. Merge sort and quick sort are written this way, so learn this tool first.</p>
      <div class="def"><p class="note-label">Recursion</p><p><b>Recursion</b> is when a method calls itself to solve a smaller version of the same problem. Each <b>call</b> (one run of the method) gets its own copy of the parameters.</p></div>
      <p>Every recursive method has two parts:</p>
      <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Meaning</th><th>With the papers</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b class="c-base">base case</b></td><td>a job so small that the method answers it at once, <b>without</b> calling itself; this is what makes it stop</td><td>"Only 1 paper? It is sorted. Give it back."</td></tr>
          <tr><td><b class="c-rec">recursive case</b></td><td>the method calls itself on a <b>smaller</b> job</td><td>"Split it; a helper takes each half."</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>Here is a tiny recursive method. It prints a number, then calls itself with half of that number, until the number is 0. Green is the base case and blue is the recursive case:</p>
      <p class="rg-key small"><span class="c-base">■ base case</span> <span class="c-rec">■ recursive case</span></p>
      <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiR(halvesCode, [2, 3, 4], [6])} /></pre>
      <p>One small Java fact first: dividing two <code>int</code>s drops the decimal part.</p>
      <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(intDiv)} /></pre>
      <p>To keep track of the calls, Java uses the <b>call stack</b>:</p>
      <div class="def"><p class="note-label">Call stack</p><p>The <b>call stack</b> is Java's list of method calls that have started but not finished. A new call goes on <b>top</b>; the call under it <b>waits</b>. When the top call finishes, it leaves, and Java goes back to the call below it, right where it left off. (It works like the stack of plates in the <a href={base + '/itc120/stacks/'}>Stacks</a> lesson.)</p></div>
      <CodeWalk id="cw-halves" layout="stack" code={halvesCode} steps={halvesSteps()} cap="Walkthrough: printHalves(20)" hint="Use Next to watch the calls pile up on the stack, then leave it." />
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Mistake: no base case</p>
        <p><b>Situation:</b> a student leaves out the <code>if</code>:</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(noBase)} /></pre>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig"><Fragment set:html={noBasePic()} /><figcaption>0 / 2 is 0, so <code>printHalves(0)</code> calls <code>printHalves(0)</code> forever.</figcaption></figure>
        <p><b>What goes wrong:</b> every call makes another call, and none of them finishes. The call stack fills up memory, and Java stops the program with a <code>StackOverflowError</code>.</p>
        <p><b>Fix:</b> always write the base case first, and make sure each recursive call has a <b>smaller</b> job that reaches it.</p>
      </div>
      <p class="small muted">Good to know: the <a href={base + '/itc120/recursion/'}>Recursion</a> lesson (midterm) goes deeper, with recursion on trees. For this lesson, you only need to follow calls on the stack.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="merge">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 3 · Sort 1</p>
      <h2>Merge sort: split, then merge</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="def"><p class="note-label">Merge sort</p><p><b>Merge sort</b> splits the array into two halves, sorts each half with merge sort (recursion), and then <b>merges</b> the two sorted halves into one sorted array. A part with one value is the base case: it is already sorted.</p></div>
      <div class="sub" id="merge-step">
        <h3>The key step: merging two sorted piles</h3>
        <p>Two helpers hand back two sorted piles, face up: 10, 14, 29 and 13, 37. To make one sorted pile, you only ever look at the <b>top</b> paper of each pile: take the smaller one, and repeat. When one pile is empty, put the rest of the other pile at the end. Those are the <b>leftovers</b>, and they need no more comparisons.</p>
        <StepShow id="show-merge-one" cap="Merging 10, 14, 29 with 13, 37, by hand" steps={mergeOneSteps()} />
        <p>Merging two piles with a total of n values takes at most n - 1 comparisons. That is the cheap part.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sub" id="merge-hand">
        <h3>The whole sort by hand</h3>
        <p>On paper, write the <b>split</b> rows going down until every part has one value, then the <b>merge</b> rows going back up. When a part has an odd number of values, the left half gets the extra one (like <code>mid</code> in binary search: <code>(low + high) / 2</code>, rounded down).</p>
        <StepShow id="show-merge-tree" cap="Merge sort on 29, 10, 14, 37, 13, by hand" steps={mergeTreeSteps()} />
      </div>
      <div class="sub" id="merge-java">
        <h3>Merge sort in Java</h3>
        <p>The code has two methods. <code>mergeSort</code> does the splitting with recursion; <code>merge</code> does the merging. Both work on a <b>part</b> of the array, from index <code>low</code> to index <code>high</code>, so the first call is <code>mergeSort(arr, 0, arr.length - 1)</code>. Both are <Peek label="static void" title="From the Programming refresher" html={voidDef} />.</p>
        <CodeWalk id="cw-mergesort" code={mergeSortCode} steps={mergeSortWalk()} cap="Walkthrough: mergeSort on 29, 10, 14" hint="Use Next to follow the calls. The array is short so you can see every call." />
        <p>Now the <code>merge</code> method, on the last merge of the five scores. It is long, but it is just the "two piles" steps from above, in code:</p>
        <CodeWalk id="cw-merge" code={mergeCode} steps={mergeWalk()} cap="Walkthrough: merge(arr, 0, 2, 4) on 10, 14, 29 | 13, 37" hint="Use Next to go step by step, or tap a line." />
        <p>Some lines are new. Here is how to read them aloud:</p>
        <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Code</th><th>Read it as</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><code>int[] temp = new int[high - low + 1];</code></td><td>"Make a new, empty <code>int</code> array called temp, with one slot for each value from low to high." A new <code>int</code> array starts full of 0s.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>while (i &lt;= mid &amp;&amp; j &lt;= high)</code></td><td>"While BOTH halves still have values."</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>if (arr[i] &lt;= arr[j])</code></td><td>"If the left front is smaller or equal, take it." On a tie, the left one goes first.</td></tr>
            <tr><td><code>arr[low + k] = temp[k];</code></td><td>"Copy slot k of temp back into the matching slot of arr." temp starts at 0, but the part of arr starts at <code>low</code>.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
      </div>
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Mistake: forgetting the leftovers</p>
        <p><b>Situation:</b> a student writes only the main loop and the copy-back loop:</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(badLeftover)} /></pre>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig"><Fragment set:html={leftoverBadPic()} /><figcaption>The main loop stops when the left half is used up, so 37 is never copied.</figcaption></figure>
        <p><b>What goes wrong:</b> when one half runs out, the other half still has values. They never reach <code>temp</code>, so its last slot keeps its starting 0, and 37 is replaced by 0.</p>
        <p><b>Fix:</b> after the main loop, copy what is left of the left half, then what is left of the right half. Only one of the two loops will actually copy something.</p>
      </div>
      <p>One cost to remember: merge sort needs the extra array <code>temp</code>. For n values, that is extra memory for up to n values.</p>
      <div class="break-note">Good place to take a break.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="quick">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 4 · Sort 2</p>
      <h2>Quick sort: pick a pivot</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Merge sort does the easy part first (splitting) and the work at the end (merging). Quick sort does it the other way around: the work comes first, so nothing needs merging at the end.</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={pivotTables()} /><figcaption>After one round, the pivot is in its final place, and each table is a smaller sorting job.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="def"><p class="note-label">Quick sort, pivot, partition</p><p><b>Quick sort</b> picks one value, the <b>pivot</b>, and <b>partitions</b> the array: it moves every value smaller than the pivot to the pivot's left and every other value to its right. The pivot is then in its final place. Quick sort then sorts the left part and the right part the same way (recursion). A part with 0 or 1 values is the base case.</p></div>
      <p>There are many ways to pick a pivot. In this lesson, the pivot is always the <b>last value</b> of the part. By hand, partitioning goes like this:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>The pivot is the last value. Put a marker <code>i</code> at the first index: the next small value goes there.</li>
        <li>Walk <code>j</code> from the first index to the one before the pivot. Each time <code>arr[j]</code> is smaller than the pivot, swap it into index <code>i</code> and move <code>i</code> one step right.</li>
        <li>At the end, swap the pivot into index <code>i</code>. It is now in its final place.</li>
        <li>Do the same for the part left of the pivot and the part right of it.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Step through it. Gold is the pivot, teal values are smaller than the pivot, blue ones are not, and green values are in their final place:</p>
      <StepShow id="show-quick" cap="Quick sort on 29, 10, 14, 37, 13, by hand" steps={quickHand} />
      <p>Each pivot splits its part into "smaller" and "bigger", like a tree. On paper, you can draw it like this:</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig"><Fragment set:html={quickTreePic()} /><figcaption>Pivot 13, then pivot 29. Parts with one value need no work.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="sub" id="quick-java">
        <h3>Quick sort in Java</h3>
        <p><code>quickSort</code> is short, because <code>partition</code> does the work. Compare it with <code>mergeSort</code>: here the work (partition) comes <b>before</b> the two recursive calls.</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiR(quickSortCode, [2, 3, 4], [6, 7])} /></pre>
        <p>The first call is <code>quickSort(arr, 0, arr.length - 1)</code>. Here is the first partition of the five scores:</p>
        <CodeWalk id="cw-partition" code={partitionCode} steps={partitionWalk()} cap="Walkthrough: partition(arr, 0, 4) on 29, 10, 14, 37, 13" hint="Use Next to go step by step, or tap a line." />
        <p><code>partition</code> changes the array <b>and</b> returns a value: the pivot's index, so <code>quickSort</code> knows where the two parts are.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Mistake: including the pivot again</p>
        <p><b>Situation:</b> a student writes the first recursive call with <code>p</code> instead of <code>p - 1</code>:</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(badQuick)} /></pre>
        <p><b>What goes wrong:</b> the pivot is already in place, but it is sorted again. On the part 10, 13, the pivot 13 stays at the end, so <code>quickSort(arr, 0, 1)</code> calls <code>quickSort(arr, 0, 1)</code>: the same job, forever. The program ends with a <code>StackOverflowError</code>, just like a missing base case.</p>
        <p><b>Fix:</b> leave the pivot out: <code>low</code> to <code>p - 1</code> and <code>p + 1</code> to <code>high</code>. Each call must get a <b>smaller</b> job.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sub" id="quick-worst">
        <h3>Quick sort's weak spot</h3>
        <p>Quick sort is fast when each pivot splits its part into two halves of about the same size. But what if the array is <b>already sorted</b>?</p>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig"><Fragment set:html={quickWorstPic()} /><figcaption>Each partition only removes the pivot itself, so it does as much work as bubble sort.</figcaption></figure>
        <p>With our last-value pivot, a sorted array of 1,000 values takes <b>{fmt(sorted1000.quick)}</b> comparisons, the same as bubble sort. Real programs avoid this by picking the <b>middle</b> value or a <b>random</b> value as the pivot (and swapping it to the end first). Then a bad split is very unlikely.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="break-note">Good place to take a break.</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="heap">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 5 · Sort 3</p>
      <h2>Heap sort: the biggest on top</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Selection sort finds the smallest value by checking every value, pass after pass. That is what makes it slow. Heap sort keeps the values in a shape where the biggest is always on top, like a sports fest bracket:</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig illus"><Fragment set:html={sportsFest()} /><figcaption>After the champion leaves, only the matches on the champion's path need a replay, not the whole fest.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="sub" id="tree-words">
        <h3>First: a tree of values</h3>
        <p>The bracket is a <b>tree</b>: values connected from the top down. You will study trees fully in the <a href={base + '/itc120/trees/'}>Trees</a> lesson (midterm); for heap sort you only need these words:</p>
        <div class="def"><p class="note-label">Tree words</p><p>Each circle is a <b>node</b> (it holds one value). The top node is the <b>root</b>. A node directly below another is its <b>child</b>; the one above is its <b>parent</b>. A node with no children is a <b>leaf</b>. In a <b>binary tree</b>, every node has at most 2 children.</p></div>
        <p>A heap is stored in a plain array. Read the array from left to right and fill the tree row by row, from left to right: index 0 is the root, index 1 and 2 are its children, and so on. No links are needed, because a simple rule finds every child:</p>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig"><Fragment set:html={heapIndexPic()} /><figcaption>The same five values as a tree (top) and as an array (bottom).</figcaption></figure>
        <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>For the node at index i</th><th>Index</th><th>Check with index 1</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>left child</td><td><code>2 * i + 1</code></td><td>3</td></tr>
            <tr><td>right child</td><td><code>2 * i + 2</code></td><td>4</td></tr>
            <tr><td>parent</td><td><code>(i - 1) / 2</code> (rounded down)</td><td>0</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <div class="def"><p class="note-label">Max-heap</p><p>A <b>max-heap</b> is a binary tree, filled row by row from the left, where every parent is <b>greater than or equal to</b> its children. So the biggest value is always at the root, index 0.</p></div>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig"><Fragment set:html={heapOrNotPic()} /><figcaption>Only parent and child are compared. 31 may be bigger than 25 on the other side.</figcaption></figure>
      </div>
      <div class="sub" id="heap-hand">
        <h3>Heap sort by hand</h3>
        <p>The main move is <b>sift down</b>: a value that is too small for its spot swaps with its <b>bigger</b> child, again and again, until it is at least as big as its children (or has none). Heap sort has two steps:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><b>Build a max-heap.</b> Sift down every parent, starting from the last parent (index n / 2 - 1) back to the root.</li>
          <li><b>Take the biggest out, again and again.</b> Swap the root (the biggest) with the last value of the heap. That value is now in its final place, at the end, and leaves the heap. Sift the new root down to repair the heap. Repeat until the heap has one value.</li>
        </ol>
        <p>It is selection sort's idea ("find the biggest, put it at the end"), but the heap finds the biggest with a few swaps along one path instead of a whole pass. Watch the tree and the array change together:</p>
        <StepShow id="show-heap" cap="Heap sort on 29, 10, 14, 37, 13, by hand" steps={heapHand} />
      </div>
      <div class="sub" id="heap-java">
        <h3>Heap sort in Java</h3>
        <p><code>heapSort</code> follows the two steps. It uses no recursion: the sifting is a <code>while</code> loop.</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(heapSortCode)} /></pre>
        <p>Here is <code>siftDown</code>, right after 37 was swapped to the end (the heap is now index 0 to 3):</p>
        <CodeWalk id="cw-sift" code={siftDownCode} steps={siftWalk()} cap="Walkthrough: siftDown(arr, 0, 4) on 13, 29, 14, 10 | 37" hint="Use Next to go step by step, or tap a line." />
        <p>The parameter <code>size</code> matters: values at index <code>size</code> and beyond are already sorted, so <code>siftDown</code> must not treat them as children.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="trap">
        <p class="note-label">Mistake: the wrong child formula</p>
        <p><b>Situation:</b> a student remembers "children at 2i and 2i + 1" from a book:</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(badHeap)} /></pre>
        <p><b>What goes wrong:</b> that formula is for heaps that start at index 1. In a Java array, the root is at index 0, and 2 × 0 = 0: the root would be its own child, and the tree is read wrong.</p>
        <p><b>Fix:</b> with the root at index 0, use <code>2 * i + 1</code> and <code>2 * i + 2</code>. Check the formula on index 0: its children must be 1 and 2 (see the <Peek label="index picture" title="Heap index rule" html={treeDef} />).</p>
      </div>
      <p class="small muted">Good to know: some books store the heap starting at index 1 (children 2i and 2i + 1). Both are correct; just use the formula that matches where the root is. Heaps are also used outside sorting, as <b>priority queues</b>: Java's <code>PriorityQueue</code> is a heap.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

