  <section class="chapter wrap" id="work">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 7 · Why this matters</p>
      <h2>How much work do they save?</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>First, the five quiz scores, counted from the lab (and from running the Java code). Here are the comparisons:</p>
      <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Array</th><th>Bubble</th><th>Insertion</th><th>Merge</th><th>Quick</th><th>Heap</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>quiz scores</td><td>{c5.scores.bubble}</td><td>{c5.scores.insertion}</td><td>{c5.scores.merge}</td><td>{c5.scores.quick}</td><td>{c5.scores.heap}</td></tr>
          <tr><td>already sorted</td><td>{c5.sorted.bubble}</td><td>{c5.sorted.insertion}</td><td>{c5.sorted.merge}</td><td>{c5.sorted.quick}</td><td>{c5.sorted.heap}</td></tr>
          <tr><td>reversed</td><td>{c5.reversed.bubble}</td><td>{c5.reversed.insertion}</td><td>{c5.reversed.merge}</td><td>{c5.reversed.quick}</td><td>{c5.reversed.heap}</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>With 5 values, there is not much difference. Heap sort even does more. The difference shows with <b>more values</b>. Here are the comparisons on random arrays (the same arrays for every sort):</p>
      <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th>Values</th><th>Bubble / selection</th><th>Insertion</th><th>Merge</th><th>Quick</th><th>Heap</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          {race.map((r) => <tr><td>{fmt(r.n)}</td><td>{fmt(r.bubble)}</td><td>{fmt(r.insertion)}</td><td>{fmt(r.merge)}</td><td>{fmt(r.quick)}</td><td>{fmt(r.heap)}</td></tr>)}
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p>For 10,000 values, bubble sort makes about {fmt(Math.round(race[3].bubble / race[3].merge))} times as many comparisons as merge sort. Why so big a gap? Look at how merge sort splits:</p>
      <figure class="fig illus-fig"><Fragment set:html={levelsPic()} /><figcaption>The number of levels is how many times you can halve n until you reach 1.</figcaption></figure>
      <div class="def"><p class="note-label">log₂ n ("log base 2 of n")</p><p><b>log₂ n</b> is the number of times you can halve n until you reach 1. log₂ 8 = 3 (8 → 4 → 2 → 1), log₂ 1,024 = 10, and log₂ 1,000,000 is about 20. It grows very slowly. (You met the same halving in <a href={base + '/itc120/searching/'}>binary search</a>.)</p></div>
      <p>Merge sort has about log₂ n levels, and the merges on each level touch all n values. So its work is about <b>n × log₂ n</b>. For 1,000,000 values: about 1,000,000 × 20 = 20 million, against about 500 billion for bubble sort. Quick sort (with good pivots) and heap sort grow the same way, only with different extra amounts.</p>
      <p>Here is the full summary of the three sorts in this lesson:</p>
      <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
        <thead><tr><th></th><th>Merge sort</th><th>Quick sort</th><th>Heap sort</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Usual work</td><td>about n × log₂ n</td><td>about n × log₂ n, often the fastest in practice</td><td>about n × log₂ n</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Worst case</td><td>still about n × log₂ n</td><td>about n × n / 2 (bad pivots, like sorted data with a last-value pivot)</td><td>still about n × log₂ n</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Extra memory</td><td>a helper array of up to n values</td><td>none for values (only the call stack)</td><td>none</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Stable?</td><td>yes</td><td>no</td><td>no</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table></div>
      <p class="small muted">Good to know: books write this growth as <b>O(n log n)</b> ("order n log n"). Merge sort and heap sort are O(n log n) even in the worst case; quick sort is O(n log n) on average and O(n²) in the worst case. You met O(n²) in Basic sorting.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="choose">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 8 · Choosing</p>
      <h2>Stability, choosing, and Java's own sort</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <div class="sub" id="stable">
        <h3>Which ones are stable?</h3>
        <p>Remember <Peek label="stable sorting" title="From Basic sorting" html={stableDef} />? Here is a short class list, already in A-to-Z order. Ana and Cris tie at 85. Here it is sorted by grade with each sort:</p>
        <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Sort</th><th>Result (by grade)</th><th>Ana still before Cris?</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>start (A to Z)</td><td>Ana 85, Ben 80, Cris 85</td><td></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Merge sort</td><td>Ben 80, Ana 85, Cris 85</td><td>✓ yes (stable)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Quick sort</td><td>Ben 80, Cris 85, Ana 85</td><td>✗ no</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Heap sort</td><td>Ben 80, Cris 85, Ana 85</td><td>✗ no</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
        <p>Merge sort is stable because on a tie it takes from the <b>left</b> half (<code>&lt;=</code>). In quick sort, Ben (smaller than the pivot Cris) swaps with Ana, and then the pivot swaps into Ana's new spot, which throws Ana behind Cris. In heap sort, the root Ana is swapped straight to the end, past Cris. Long jumps like these can carry a value past an equal one.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sub" id="which">
        <h3>Which sort when?</h3>
        <p>Here are all six sorts from the two lessons, by situation:</p>
        <div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="tbl">
          <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Good choice</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Big data, sorted in memory, fastest on average</td><td>Quick sort (with a middle or random pivot)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Records with ties whose order must stay (students by grade)</td><td>Merge sort (stable)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Data too big for memory, or a linked list</td><td>Merge sort (it only reads each part from front to back)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Speed must be guaranteed and there is no room for an extra array</td><td>Heap sort</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Small or almost-sorted data</td><td>Insertion sort (fast sorts use it for small parts too)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>A real Java program</td><td><code>Arrays.sort</code></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table></div>
      </div>
      <div class="sub" id="java-sort">
        <h3>What <code>Arrays.sort</code> uses</h3>
        <p>Java's own sort, which you met in Basic sorting, picks a method by the kind of array:</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hi(sortedCalls)} /></pre>
        <p>For numbers (<code>int[]</code>, <code>double[]</code>), Java uses a <b>dual-pivot quick sort</b> (two pivots, three parts). Equal numbers look the same, so stability does not matter. For objects (<code>String[]</code>, records), it uses a <b>stable merge sort</b> called TimSort, so ties keep their order.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="sub" id="program">
        <h3>All three in one program</h3>
        <p>Here are the three sorts in one runnable file. Run it, and check that each line prints the sorted scores:</p>
        <pre class="code"><code set:html={hiN(full)} /></pre>
        <TryIt />
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="chapter wrap" id="practice">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 9 · Practice</p>
      <h2>Practice</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Work these out on paper first, then check the answers. Use the array <code>&#123;8, 3, 5, 1, 9, 2&#125;</code> for problems 1 to 3.</p>
      <ol>
        <li><b>Merge sort.</b> Draw the split rows and the merge rows. How many comparisons does the last merge make?</li>
        <li><b>Quick sort.</b> Do the first partition (pivot = last value). Write the array after it, and the pivot's index.</li>
        <li><b>Heap sort.</b> Build the max-heap. Write the array after step 1, and draw it as a tree.</li>
        <li><b>Recursion.</b> What does <code>printHalves(13)</code> print? How many calls are made in total?</li>
        <li><b>Which sort?</b> (a) A registrar sorts 30,000 student records by course, and students in the same course must stay in ID order. (b) A small device has almost no free memory but must sort 50,000 sensor readings with guaranteed speed.</li>
      </ol>
      <details class="reveal"><summary>Show the answers</summary><div class="flow">
        <p class="small muted">For self-study, not for grading.</p>
        <p><b>1.</b> Here are the rows. The last merge joins 3, 5, 8 with 1, 2, 9: 3 vs 1 (take 1), 3 vs 2 (take 2), 3 vs 9 (take 3), 5 vs 9 (take 5), 8 vs 9 (take 8), then 9 is a leftover: <b>5 comparisons</b>.</p>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig"><Fragment set:html={practiceTree} /></figure>
        <p><b>2.</b> Pivot 2. Only 1 is smaller: it swaps into index 0 (with 8). Then the pivot swaps into index 1. The array is <b>1, 2, 5, 8, 9, 3</b> and the pivot index is <b>1</b> (5 comparisons).</p>
        <figure class="fig illus-fig"><Fragment set:html={pQuickPic} /></figure>
        <p><b>3.</b> Start at the last parent, index 2 (5): its only child is 2, so nothing moves. Index 1 (3): its children are 1 and 9; swap with 9. Index 0 (8): its children are 9 and 5; swap with 9, then 8 is bigger than its new children 1 and 3, so it stops. The max-heap is <b>9, 8, 5, 1, 3, 2</b>.</p>
