  <section class="chapter wrap" id="check">
    <div class="chapter-head">
      <p class="chapter-num">Part 10 · Check yourself</p>
      <h2>Self-check</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="flow">
      <p>Not graded. Tap an answer to see if you got it, and read why.</p>
      <Quiz id="quiz-sort-adv" questions={quiz} />
      <div class="remember">
        <p class="note-label">Key takeaways</p>
        <ol>
          <li><b>Recursion</b>: a method calls itself on a smaller job. The <b>base case</b> stops it; the <b>call stack</b> keeps track of the calls.</li>
          <li><b>Merge sort</b>: split in half until one value is left, then merge sorted parts by taking the smaller front value. Stable, but needs an extra array.</li>
          <li><b>Quick sort</b>: partition around a pivot (smaller left, the rest right); the pivot is then in its final place. Fast in practice, but slow with bad pivots.</li>
          <li><b>Heap sort</b>: build a max-heap (children of i at 2i + 1 and 2i + 2), then swap the biggest to the end and sift down, again and again. No extra array, not stable.</li>
          <li>All three need about <b>n × log₂ n</b> comparisons (quick sort: usually), far fewer than the basic sorts' n × n / 2 on big data.</li>
          <li>In real programs, use <code>Arrays.sort</code>: quick sort for numbers, stable merge sort for objects.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <p><b>Next lesson:</b> <a href={base + '/itc120/searching/'}>Linear and binary search</a>, where sorted data pays off.</p>
      <div class="refs small muted">
        <p class="note-label">References</p>
        <ol>
          <li>R. Sedgewick and K. Wayne, <i>Algorithms</i>, 4th ed. Addison-Wesley, 2011, Sections 2.2 (mergesort), 2.3 (quicksort), and 2.4 (heapsort). <a href="https://algs4.cs.princeton.edu/20sorting/">algs4.cs.princeton.edu</a></li>
          <li>T. H. Cormen, C. E. Leiserson, R. L. Rivest, and C. Stein, <i>Introduction to Algorithms</i>, 3rd ed. MIT Press, 2009, Chapters 2.3, 6, and 7 (the last-value pivot partition is from Chapter 7).</li>
          <li>A. Bhargava, <i>Grokking Algorithms</i>. Manning, 2016, Chapters 3 (recursion) and 4 (divide and conquer, quicksort).</li>
          <li>Oracle, "Class Arrays," Java SE 21 API (sort: dual-pivot quicksort for primitives; stable, adaptive mergesort for objects). <a href="https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/21/docs/api/java.base/java/util/Arrays.html">docs.oracle.com</a></li>
          <li>ITC 120 Course Syllabus (Advanced sorting: implement merge, quick, and heap sort; evaluate time and space; assess when each is most effective), Davao Oriental State University.</li>
